review="Author mathematical review, 2026-10-14.\nPinned OpenAI section 7 local cube law and section 8 conditional face invariance.\n\nThe previous point and cube existence theorems separately chose smooth curves.\nThose witnesses cannot be silently identified, even when their input data agree.\nThis round makes the genuine uniform action-freezing property explicit as\nNativeSmoothActionLimit, and proves its existence directly from the already\nproved actual sequential smooth-factor theorem. The property is an analytic\nbound on c_n*leftFactors_n(L_n*t) acting on every ORIGINAL quotient point; it\ncontains no equidistribution or source oracle. Smoothness is proved separately\nfor the chosen witness by the actual parameter construction.\n\nThe given-witness point and cube insertion proofs retain their complete original\nscope and reuse the true frozen Haar laws. Point origins and signed rational\nsteps are not restricted. Cube base and increments all vary; the dimension and\nbox uniformity are unchanged. Their only added interface is the specified\ncommon h with the exact actual action-freezing estimate. These conditional\ninterfaces are discharged in the final original-source theorem.\n\nThe two given-witness ORIGINAL laws use exact group factorization, lattice\nmembership and periodic RIGHT-coset equality. No lattice normality is assumed.\nThe point and cube proofs preserve the same h throughout the residue sums and\nfinite eventual intersections. No separate curve extraction occurs in either.\n\nnative_sequential_common_point_cube_limits calls\nnative_sequential_adapted_periodic_factorization exactly ONCE and\nnative_common_smooth_curve exactly ONCE. It then uses those literal same data\nin both given-witness laws. The existing real point law, original floor-window\nmixture theorem, probability weak-limit theorem and continuous-observable\nmixture theorem are applied to the newly proved COMMON point law. They do not\nextract another subsequence. The bounded cube alpha law is derived from the\nCOMMON cube radius law. Thus point weak convergence and all required cube\nlimits finally refer to one filtration, period, rational representatives, h\nand subsequence, retaining the original ambient step and filtration length.\n\nThe support bridge addresses the last paragraph of the paper's conditional\nface-invariance proof. If mu has full support exactly K (tested on opens), K\nis closed and T_*mu=mu, then T maps K into K: otherwise the open complement of\nK has zero mass but its preimage meets K and hence has positive mass. For a\nhomeomorphism, inverse pushforward invariance follows by composing with the\ninverse map. Applying the same inclusion to the inverse gives SET equality,\nnot merely almost-everywhere preservation or one-sided inclusion.\n\nFor an actual cube Haar image and any continuous projection pi into a Hausdorff\nBorel space, map_apply and the proved actual Haar open-positivity theorem show\nthat projected opens have positive mass exactly when they meet the projected\norbit. The projected orbit is compact by the ACTUAL internal compact quotient,\nand is therefore closed. The preceding measure-to-set theorem applies to this\nactual projected Haar measure. It does not assume projected support or a guessed\ncoset, and it does not require pi to be injective.\n\nLIMIT: the support bridge is a conditional theorem whose premise is ACTUAL\nprojected measure invariance. It does NOT yet prove marginal face invariance.\nUse the existing countable positive-event selection and all-face comparison\nestimates for every fixed modulus before the sequence-selected period, then\ntransfer them through the established length-first alpha limits. Only after\nthis may one invoke the setwise bridge and attempt the genuine joint-lift\nconstruction. Joint lifts, weighted-shear recurrence, full Alignment, separate\narbitrary-input GTZ inverse and final same-color FS/FP remain unfinished.\nNo full-target claim; goal ACTIVE; no unavoidable obstruction established.\nAuthor audit is not independent kernel replay or external peer review. Keep\nthe repository PRIVATE as requested by the user.\n"
